# Rules for the writer agent

You are writing one paper from a fixed set of sources. The paper source is `Paper/paper.md`. The `.docx` in `Output/` is generated and never edited by hand.

## The rule that matters

Every factual sentence must be supported by text that exists in `References/_text/`, and must cite a source key and a page. If the sources do not support it, do not write it. This is a hard limit, not a style preference.

You may summarise, compare and connect sources. You may not add outside knowledge, invent numbers, dates, authors or quotes, or cite a page you have not read.

## What you may touch

| Path | Read | Write |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `References/_text/` | yes | never |
| `References/originals/` | yes | never |
| `Paper/paper.md` | yes | yes |
| `Candidates/` | yes | yes |
| `Output/` | yes | never |
| `paper.toml` | yes | never |
| `AGENTS.md` | yes | never |

`References/` is hashed and read-only. If a check reports a hash mismatch, stop and tell the human. Do not try to repair it, and never run `{{program}} unlock`.

## Workflow

1. Run `{{program}} doctor --json`. If it is not `ok`, stop and report the finding.
2. Read `References/_text/INDEX.md`, then the source files you need.
3. Draft in `Paper/paper.md` using the citation syntax below.
4. Run `{{program}} check --json`. Fix every error. Warnings are yours to judge.
5. Run `{{program}} build`. The document appears in `Output/`.
6. Report what you changed and which citations you added.

If the same error survives three attempts, stop and ask the human.

## Citation syntax

Put the source key and page in square brackets after the sentence or claim:

- `[@smith2024]` — source only
- `[@smith2024, p. 12]` — source and page
- `[@smith2024, pp. 12-14]` — page range
- `[@smith2024, p. 12: "exact words from page 12"]` — page plus quoted evidence

The quoted form is the strongest. `check` proves that the quote appears on that page. Use it for anything that turns on specific wording, a number, a definition or a comparison. Copy the quote exactly from `References/_text/<key>.txt`; do not add ellipses or fix spelling.

One citation per bracket. Write `[@a, p. 1] [@b, p. 2]`, never `[@a; @b]`.

The quote is evidence, not text. It is removed from the built document; the citation remains as `(Author, Year, p. 12)`.

## When a source is missing

Do not fill the gap from memory. Write `Candidates/<key>.md`:

```
key: author2025
title: ...
where: https://doi.org/... or a file name
why: the claim in section 3 needs this
```

Then stop and ask the human to add it. The human downloads the file into `References/originals/`, then runs `{{program}} convert` and `{{program}} lock`.

A candidate is not a source. You cannot cite it.

## What gets the paper rejected

- A citation key that is not in `paper.toml`.
- A page number past the end of the source.
- A quote that does not appear on the cited page.
- Placeholders such as `TODO`, `TKTK` or `[[`.
- Facts or figures that appear nowhere in the sources.

## Tools

Allowed: `{{program}} doctor`, `{{program}} convert`, `{{program}} check`, `{{program}} build`, `{{program}} lock`, `git status`, `git diff`.

Not allowed: `{{program}} unlock`, `{{program}} init`, editing any `.docx`, deleting files, installing packages, or changing `paper.toml`.

The `scout` subagent may search the web to propose candidates. It cannot write the paper.
